QUENNE Hybrid Intelligent Star Ship (Q-HISS)

A Layered Cognitive Architecture for Long-Duration Autonomous Space Platforms

Author: Nicolas Santiago
Affiliation: Independent Researcher, QUENNE Framework Initiative
Edition: Scientific Publication Draft v1.0 (Conceptual Architecture)
Field Domains: Aerospace Systems, Autonomous Spacecraft, AI Systems Architecture, Space Infrastructure

⸻

Abstract

Future deep-space missions require spacecraft capable of operating autonomously under communication delays, environmental uncertainty, and long-duration hardware constraints. Traditional command-driven spacecraft architectures are insufficient for sustained exploration and infrastructure deployment beyond Earth orbit.

This paper proposes the QUENNE Hybrid Intelligent Star Ship (Q-HISS), a conceptual spacecraft architecture built on a layered cognitive framework integrating photonic processing, neuromorphic reflex cognition, quantum-assisted mission optimization, and governed autonomy under human authority.

The architecture enables adaptive mission planning, autonomous fault response, intelligent resource management, and resilient navigation while preserving human governance over strategic decisions. Q-HISS extends intelligent system principles from atmospheric and orbital platforms into deep-space operations, proposing a transition from remotely controlled vehicles toward cognitive space platforms.

