1. Introduction

Modern spacecraft depend heavily on Earth-based control. However, deep-space missions introduce operational challenges:
   •   Long communication latency
   •   Limited human supervision
   •   Radiation and hardware degradation
   •   Dynamic mission conditions
   
   •   Resource management constraints

As missions expand toward lunar infrastructure, Mars logistics, and deep-space exploration, spacecraft must increasingly function as autonomous operational entities.

Q-HISS proposes an architecture where intelligence is not a subsystem but a core operational layer, allowing spacecraft to adapt, diagnose, and respond independently while remaining governed by human authority.

